Jahshaka patch 0062 — the voxelisation's per-voxel merge and its DISPATCH ORDER:
the same scene must voxelise to the same voxels in every process.

SOURCE **and** MEDIA. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh`
after pulling this (the source half compiles; the media half is staged by Studio
from the SOURCE tree, so the patch loop must have run in that tree either way).
Files: `Components/Hlms/Pbs/{include,src}/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.{h,cpp}`,
`Components/Hlms/Pbs/{include,src}/Vct/OgreVctMaterial.{h,cpp}`,
`Samples/Media/VCT/Voxelizer_piece_cs.any`. It shares the two VctVoxelizer files
with patch 0061 (different functions, applied in stack order).

DEFECT 1, THE MERGE (media). `Voxelizer_piece_cs.any`, in the branch that merges
a dispatch's triangles into a voxel another dispatch already wrote:

    voxelNormal.a = doubleSided ? 1.0f : 0.0f;
    voxelNormal.a = max( voxelNormal.a, voxelNormal.a );

The second line is a no-op — `max(x, x)` — and it is plainly a typo for
`max( flag, origNormal.a )`: every other quantity on the three lines above it is
merged with what was already there (`mixAverage3/4`), and this one alone
overwrites. So the double-sided flag at a voxel is whatever the LAST dispatch
that touched it decided, and a dispatch that adds no triangle at a voxel an
earlier one filled still enters this branch (the condition is
`accumTris + origAccumTris > 0`) and CLEARS it. The flag is read per light in
`LightInjection_piece_cs.any:103-106` — `normalVal.a > 0` lights the voxel from
both sides (`abs(NdotL)`) instead of one (`saturate(NdotL)`) — so a lost flag is
a systematically darker voxel wherever two dispatches meet: exactly the floor /
wall / prop junctions. Fixed by merging it like everything else beside it.

DEFECT 2, THE ORDER (source). Which dispatch is "last" is the iteration order of
`mBuckets`, a `std::map` whose key compares POINTERS — the compute job, the
material const buffer, the vertex and index buffers (OgreVctVoxelizer.h:64-75).
Allocation order is not a property of the scene: the same document voxelised in
the session that built it and in the session that reopened it walks its buckets
in a different order, and with defect 1 that is a different set of voxels. It is
not correctable by anything downstream — a re-injection re-lights the voxels it
has, and a re-solve in the SAME process reproduces the same addresses — which is
why it survives every comparison of boxes, item counts, attach sets and document
fields. Upstream knows the class: `OGRE_FORCE_VCT_VOXELIZER_DETERMINISTIC` in the
same header orders `MeshPtr`s by NAME for the same reason, and does not cover
this map. Fixed by ordering the buckets by WHAT THEY ARE — the job's variant bits
(which also decide the vertex/index buffers and the texture pool) and the
material's POOL and SLOT — with the old pointer comparisons left underneath as a
tie-break nothing reaches. `VctMaterial::DatablockConversionResult` gains the
pool index it already knew; nothing else changes.

WHAT EACH HALF IS WORTH, MEASURED (lane PHOTON-E2, round 2;
spikes/photon-e2/FINDINGS-history.md). The lane's two red suites were NOT this:
they were patch 0061's stale octants, and they go green on 0061 alone (the media
half alone moves neither: 52 vs 59 before and after on the field-off probe, and
the shipped configuration is already 0 with 0061). This patch is therefore a
DEFECT FIXED ON ITS MERITS with a determinism suite behind it, not a bug fix with
a red to its name — and the honest reason to take it is that a renderer whose
output depends on `malloc` cannot be reasoned about at all.

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` on a tree carrying 0001-0061 and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane
worktree. Suite: `gi.cascade_determinism` (the same scene voxelised twice, in
two orders, must be the same picture). `--engine-selftest`: the media half moves
the flag at junction voxels, so the hash moves with it — the lane's report
carries the A/B and the pixel count.

